== 3. Lack of Preparation

=== The third #emph[device] that Satan has to keep poor sinners from believing in Christ, from embracing Christ, from resting on Christ, etc. is,

BY suggesting to them the lack of such and such preparations and qualifications. Satan says, “You are not prepared to receive Christ; you are not humbled and justified in this way and that; you are not heartsick of sin; you have not been under horrors and terrors as such and such people have been. You must wait until you are prepared and qualified to receive the Lord Jesus,” etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider seriously that those who have not been prepared and qualified in this way and that, as Satan suggests, have received Christ, believed in Christ, and been saved by Christ. Matthew #footnote[Matt 9:9.] was called while sitting at the tax booth, and such power went along with Christ’s call that it made him follow. We do not read of any horrors or terrors, etc., that he was under before he was called by Christ. Tell me, what preparations and qualifications were found in Zacchaeus, Paul, the#footnote[Luke 19:9. Acts 16. chap.] jailer, and Lydia before their conversion? God brings some in by the sweet and still voice of the gospel, and usually those who are brought in to Christ this way are the sweetest, humblest, choicest and most fruitful Christians. God is a free agent, to work by law or gospel, by smiles or frowns, by presenting hell or heaven to sinners’ souls. God thunders from Mount Sinai on some souls, and conquers them by thundering. God speaks to others in a still voice, and by that conquers them. You#super[+] who are brought to Christ by the law, do not judge and condemn those who are brought to Christ by the#footnote[Rom 14.] gospel; and you#super[+] who are brought to Christ by the gospel, do not despise those who are brought to Christ by the law. Some are brought to Christ by fire, storms and tempests, others by easier and gentler breezes of the Spirit. The Spirit is free in the works of#footnote[John 3:8.] conversion, and like the wind, it blows when, where and how it pleases. Thrice happy are those souls that are brought to Christ, whether it is on a winter’s night or on a summer’s day.

The second #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to dwell solemnly on the following Scriptures, which clearly show that poor sinners who are not prepared and qualified in this way and that to meet with Christ, to receive and embrace the Lord Jesus Christ, may nevertheless believe in Christ, and rest and lean on him for happiness and blessedness, according to the gospel. Read the first chapter of Proverbs from verse 20 to the end, the first eleven verses of Proverbs 8, the first six verses of Proverbs 9, the first fourteen verses of Ezekiel 16, John 3:14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 36, and Revelation 3:15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20. Here the Lord Jesus Christ stands knocking at the Laodiceans’ door. He would gladly have them dine with him, and dine with them himself; that is, that he and they might have intimate communion and fellowship with one another.

Now tell me, what preparations or qualifications did these Laodiceans have to receive Christ? Surely none. For they were lukewarm; they were neither cold nor hot; they were wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked. And yet Christ, to show his free grace and his stooping love, invites the very worst of sinners to open to him, though they were in no way prepared or qualified like this to receive him, etc.

The third #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider seriously that the Lord nowhere in all of Scripture requires such and such preparations#footnote[Rom 4:5. God justifies the ungodly.] and qualifications before men come to Christ, before they believe in Christ, or receive or embrace the Lord Jesus Christ. Believing, believing in Christ, is the great thing that God presses on sinners throughout Scripture, as all who know anything of Scripture know.

#emph[Objection.] But does not Christ say, “Come #footnote[Matt 11:28.] to Me, all you#super[+] who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you#super[+] rest”? To this I will give these three answers.

1\. Though the invitation is to those who #emph[labor and are heavy laden,] yet the promise of giving rest is made to coming, to believing.

2\. I answer that all this Scripture proves and shows is that those who labor under sin as under a heavy burden,#footnote[Matt 11:28 opened and explained.] and who are laden with the guilt of sin and the sense of God’s displeasure, ought to come to Christ for rest. But it does not prove that only such must come to Christ, nor that all men must be burdened like this, and laden with the sense of their sins and the wrath of God, before they come to Christ. When poor sinners are under the sense of sin and the wrath of God, they are prone to run from one created thing to another, and from duty to duty, and from ordinance to ordinance, to find rest; and if they could find it in anything or any created thing, Christ would never hear from them. But here the Lord sweetly invites them, and to encourage them, he binds himself to give them rest. “Come,” says Christ, “and I will give you#super[+] rest. I will not show you#super[+] #emph[rest,] nor merely tell you#super[+] of #emph[rest,] but I will give you#super[+] rest. I am faithfulness itself and cannot lie: I will give you#super[+] rest. I, who have the greatest power to give it, the greatest will to give it, the greatest right to give it: come, laden sinners, and I will give you#super[+] rest.” Rest is the most desirable good, the most suitable good, and to you#super[+] the greatest good. “Come,” says Christ; that is, “Believe in me and I will give you#super[+] rest. I will give you#super[+] peace with God and peace with conscience; I will turn your#super[+] storm into an everlasting calm; I will give you#super[+] such rest as the world can neither give to you#super[+] nor take from you#super[+].”

3\. I answer: no one Scripture expresses the whole mind of Christ. Therefore just compare this one Scripture with the various Scriptures laid down in the second #emph[remedy] mentioned above, and it will clearly appear that though men are not burdened and laden with their sins like this, and filled with horror and terror, yet they may come to Christ; they may receive and embrace the Lord Jesus Christ.

The fourth and last #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider that all the trouble for sin, all the sorrow, shame and mourning, etc., that is acceptable to God, delightful to God and prevails with God, flows from faith in Christ, as the stream does from the fountain, as the branch does from the root, as the effect does from the cause. Zech 12:10: “They will look on #footnote[Zech 12:10.] Me, the One they have pierced. They will mourn for Him.” All gospel mourning#footnote[Rom 14:23.] flows from believing: they will first look, and then mourn. All who know anything know this, that “everything that is not from faith is sin.” Until men have faith in Christ, their best services are only glorious sins.
